                "content": "# Three-Point Testcross with Viability Selection\n\n## Part 1: Identifying Affected Classes and Correcting Counts\n\nThe offspring genotype at each locus is the female gamete allele combined with the male's *abc* allele. An offspring is **a/a; c/c** if and only if the female gamete carries both *a* and *c*.\n\n| Gamete | Offspring genotype at A | At C | Is a/a; c/c? |\n|--------|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| ABC | A/a | C/c | No |\n| **abc** | **a/a** | **c/c** | **Yes** |\n| **Abc** | **a/a** | **c/c** | **Yes** |\n| aBC | a/a | C/c | No |\n| ABc | A/a | c/c | No |\n| abC | a/a | C/c | No |\n| AbC | a/a | C/c | No |\n| **aBc** | **a/a** | **c/c** | **Yes** |\n\nThe affected classes are **abc, Abc, and aBc**. Since they survive with probability 1/2, we double their observed counts:\n\n| Gamete | Observed | Corrected |\n|--------|--------:|--------:|\n| ABC | 344 | 344 |\n| abc | 170 | **340** |\n| Abc | 80 | **160** |\n| aBC | 80 | 80 |\n| ABc | 70 | 70 |\n| abC | 70 | 70 |\n| AbC | 8 | 8 |\n| aBc | 4 | **8** |\n| **Total** | **826** | **1080** |\n\nVerification: $344+340+160+80+70+70+8+8 = 1080$ \u2713\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Parental, Double-Crossover Classes, and Gene Order\n\n**Parental classes** (largest counts, matching the known haplotypes $ABC$ and $abc$):\n\n$$ABC = 344, \\qquad abc = 340$$\n\n**Rarest classes** (candidates for DCO): $AbC = 8$ and $aBc = 8$.\n\nTo determine gene order, I test which arrangement places $AbC$ and $aBc$ as the DCO products. A double crossover flips the middle gene relative to both flanking genes.\n\n| Hypothesized order | Predicted DCO classes | Observed counts | Match? |\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| A\u2013B\u2013C | AbC, aBc | 8, 8 | \u2713 (rarest) |\n| B\u2013A\u2013C | aBC, Abc | 80, 160 | \u2717 |\n| A\u2013C\u2013B | ABc, abC | 70, 70 | \u2717 |\n\n**Gene order: A \u2013 B \u2013 C**\n\nFull classification of the corrected data:\n\n| Class type | Gametes | Corrected counts |\n|:---|:---:|:---:|\n| Parental | $ABC,\\; abc$ | 344, 340 |\n| SCO interval 1 (A\u2013B) | $Abc,\\; aBC$ | 160, 80 |\n| SCO interval 2 (B\u2013C) | $ABc,\\; abC$ | 70, 70 |\n| DCO | $AbC,\\; aBc$ | 8, 8 |\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: Recombination Fractions and Map Distances\n\n**Interval A\u2013B** (recombinants carry *A b* or *a B*):\n\n$$\\text{A\u2013B recombinants} = Abc + aBC + AbC + aBc = 160 + 80 + 8 + 8 = 256$$\n\n$$\\hat{r}_{AB} = \\frac{256}{1080} = \\frac{32}{135} \\approx 0.2370$$\n\n$$\\boxed{d_{AB} = 23.70 \\text{ cM}}$$\n\n**Interval B\u2013C** (recombinants carry *B c* or *b C*):\n\n$$\\text{B\u2013C recombinants} = ABc + abC + AbC + aBc = 70 + 70 + 8 + 8 = 156$$\n\n$$\\hat{r}_{BC} = \\frac{156}{1080} = \\frac{13}{90} \\approx 0.1444$$\n\n$$\\boxed{d_{BC} = 14.44 \\text{ cM}}$$\n\n---\n\n## Part 4: Recombination Fraction Between Outer Genes\n\n**Interval A\u2013C** (recombinants carry *A c* or *a C*):\n\n$$\\text{A\u2013C recombinants} = Abc + aBC + ABc + abC = 160 + 80 + 70 + 70 = 380$$\n\n$$\\hat{r}_{AC} = \\frac{380}{1080} = \\frac{19}{54} \\approx 0.3519$$\n\n**Why $\\hat{r}_{AC} \\neq \\hat{r}_{AB} + \\hat{r}_{BC}$:**\n\n$$\\hat{r}_{AB} + \\hat{r}_{BC} = \\frac{256 + 156}{1080} = \\frac{412}{1080} \\approx 0.3815$$\n\nThe difference is:\n\n$$\\hat{r}_{AB} + \\hat{r}_{BC} - \\hat{r}_{AC} = \\frac{412 - 380}{1080} = \\frac{32}{1080} = \\frac{2 \\times 16}{1080}$$\n\nThis equals **twice the DCO frequency**. A double-crossover event is counted as a recombinant in *each* of the two adjacent intervals (contributing to both $\\hat{r}_{AB}$ and $\\hat{r}_{BC}$), but in the outer genes the two crossovers **cancel**, restoring the original *A\u2013C* coupling. Thus DCO gametes ($AbC$, $aBc$) are *non-recombinant* for A\u2013C but *recombinant* for both A\u2013B and B\u2013C. The sum of the two adjacent fractions therefore overestimates the outer-gene recombination fraction by $2 \\times \\text{DCO frequency}$.\n\n---\n\n## Part 5: Coefficient of Coincidence and Interference (Corrected Data)\n\n**Expected DCO** under independence of crossovers in the two intervals:\n\n$$E(\\text{DCO}) = \\hat{r}_{AB} \\times \\hat{r}_{BC} \\times N = \\frac{256}{1080} \\times \\frac{156}{1080} \\times 1080 = \\frac{256 \\times 156}{1080}$$\n\n$$E(\\text{DCO}) = \\frac{39\\,936}{1080} = 36.98$$\n\n**Observed DCO** (corrected):\n\n$$\\text{Observed DCO} = 8 + 8 = 16$$\n\n**Coefficient of coincidence:**\n\n$$c = \\frac{16}{36.98} = 0.4327$$\n\n**Interference:**\n\n$$I = 1 - c = 1 - 0.4327 = 0.5673$$\n\n$$\\boxed{c \\approx 0.433, \\qquad I \\approx 0.567}$$\n\n---\n\n## Part 6: Uncorrected (Observed) Linkage Calculations\n\nTotal observed: $N = 826$\n\n**Interval A\u2013B:**\n\n$$\\text{A\u2013B recombinants} = 80 + 80 + 8 + 4 = 172$$\n\n$$\\hat{r}_{AB}^{\\,\\text{obs}} = \\frac{172}{826} = \\frac{86}{413} \\approx 0.2082 \\quad (20.82 \\text{ cM})$$\n\n**Interval B\u2013C:**\n\n$$\\text{B\u2013C recombinants} = 70 + 70 + 8 + 4 = 152$$\n\n$$\\hat{r}_{BC}^{\\,\\text{obs}} = \\frac{152}{826} = \\frac{76}{413} \\approx 0.1840 \\quad (18.40 \\text{ cM})$$\n\n**Interval A\u2013C:**\n\n$$\\text{A\u2013C recombinants} = 80 + 80 + 70 + 70 = 300$$\n\n$$\\hat{r}_{AC}^{\\,\\text{obs}} = \\frac{300}{826} = \\frac{150}{413} \\approx 0.3632 \\quad (36.32 \\text{ cM})$$\n\n**Expected DCO (uncorrected):**\n\n$$E(\\text{DCO})_{\\text{obs}} = \\frac{172 \\times 152}{826} = \\frac{26\\,144}{826} = 31.65$$\n\n**Observed DCO (uncorrected):** $8 + 4 = 12$\n\n**Coefficient of coincidence:**\n\n$$c_{\\text{obs}} = \\frac{12}{31.65} = 0.3791$$\n\n**Interference:**\n\n$$I_{\\text{obs}} = 1 - 0.3791 = 0.6209$$\n\n---\n\n## Part 7: Comparison and Explanation of Distortion\n\n### Quantitative Comparison\n\n| Parameter | Corrected | Uncorrected | Change |\n|:---|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| $r_{AB}$ | 0.2370 | 0.2082 | **underestimated by 0.0288** |\n| $r_{BC}$ | 0.1444 | 0.1840 | **overestimated by 0.0396** |\n| $r_{AC}$ | 0.3519 | 0.3632 | overestimated by 0.0113 |\n| $d_{AB}$ (cM) | 23.70 | 20.82 | \u22122.88 |\n| $d_{BC}$ (cM) | 14.44 | 18.40 | +3.96 |\n| $d_{AC}$ (cM) | 35.19 | 36.32 | +1.13 |\n| DCO (obs) | 16 | 12 | undercounted |\n| DCO (exp) | 36.98 | 31.65 | \u2014 |\n| Coefficient of coincidence | 0.433 | 0.379 | underestimated |\n| Interference | 0.567 | 0.621 | overestimated |\n\n### Why the Distortion Occurs\n\nThe viability penalty hits exactly three gamete classes: **abc** (parental), **Abc** (SCO in interval A\u2013B), and **aBc** (DCO). The critical point is that **reciprocal classes within each interval are affected unequally**:\n\n| Interval | Class reduced by \u00bd | Reciprocal class (unaffected) | Effect |\n|:---|:---:|:---:|:---|\n| A\u2013B (SCO) | *Abc* (160\u219280) | *aBC* (80, unchanged) | One SCO class halved \u2192 apparent $r_{AB}$ **decreased** |\n| B\u2013C (DCO) | *aBc* (8\u21924) | *AbC* (8, unchanged) | One DCO class halved \u2192 apparent DCO **decreased** |\n| Parental | *abc* (340\u2192170) | *ABC* (344, unchanged) | Total reduced, shifting all proportions |\n\nThe mechanism of distortion has two components:\n\n1. **Numerator effect.** For the A\u2013B interval, the recombinant class *Abc* is halved while its reciprocal *aBC* is not. This removes 80 recombinants from the numerator of $\\hat{r}_{AB}$, pulling it downward. For the B\u2013C interval, the DCO class *aBc* is halved (removing 4 from the numerator), a smaller absolute effect.\n\n2. **Denominator effect.** The total count drops from 1080 to 826 (a loss of 254), because the parental class *abc* loses 170 individuals. This reduction in the denominator inflates every recombination fraction that is *not* equally reduced in the numerator.\n\nThe **net effect** is that $r_{AB}$ is underestimated (the numerator loses proportionally more than the denominator) while $r_{BC}$ is overestimated (the denominator loses far more than the numerator, since only 4 B\u2013C recombinants are lost out of 156). The apparent map is **compressed on the left and expanded on the right**, and the apparent interference is exaggerated because the DCO count is artificially suppressed.\n\n### Why This Mimics Altered Recombination\n\nIn a real experiment without known selection, a researcher would observe:\n- Asymmetric reciprocal class sizes (e.g., *Abc* = 80 vs. *aBC* = 80 looks symmetric here, but *aBc* = 4 vs. *AbC* = 8 does not),\n- Apparent map distances that don't sum correctly to the outer-gene distance,\n- An inflated interference value.\n\nThese are the same qualitative signatures one would expect from **chromosome interference** or **sex-linked differences in recombination rate**. Without knowing about the viability selection, the data would be perfectly consistent with a model in which recombination in the B\u2013C interval is genuinely higher and interference is genuinely stronger. Post-fertilization selection thus creates a **confound between genotype-dependent survival and meiotic recombination pattern**, because both alter the observed gamete-class frequencies in ways that are mathematically indistinguishable in a single testcross experiment.",
                "reasoning_content": "Let me work through this problem step by step.\n\n**Given information:**\n- Three genes A/a, B/b, C/c on the same chromosome\n- Female heterozygous: ABC/abc\n- Testcrossed to abc/abc\n- Viability selection: offspring that are a/a; c/c survive with probability 1/2, all others survive with probability 1\n- Observed survivors are given\n\n**Step 1: Determine which gamete classes produce offspring subject to the viability penalty**\n\nThe offspring genotype is determined by the gamete from the heterozygous parent combined with the abc gamete from the testcross parent.\n\nSo the offspring genotype at each locus is:\n- Locus A: gamete allele / a\n- Locus B: gamete allele / b\n- Locus C: gamete allele / c\n\nFor an offspring to be a/a; c/c:\n- Locus A must be a/a \u2192 the gamete from the heterozygous parent must carry a\n- Locus C must be c/c \u2192 the gamete from the heterozygous parent must carry c\n\nSo the gamete must carry both 'a' and 'c'. Let me check each gamete class:\n\n- ABC: A and C \u2192 offspring is A/a; B/b; C/c \u2192 NOT a/a;c/c\n- abc: a and c \u2192 offspring is a/a; b/b; c/c \u2192 YES, a/a;c/c\n- Abc: a and c \u2192 offspring is a/a; B/b; c/c \u2192 YES, a/a;c/c\n- aBC: a and C \u2192 offspring is a/a; B/b; C/c \u2192 NOT (has C)\n- ABc: A and c \u2192 offspring is A/a; B/b; c/c \u2192 NOT (has A)\n- abC: a and C \u2192 offspring is a/a; b/b; C/c \u2192 NOT (has C)\n- AbC: a and C \u2192 offspring is a/a; B/b; C/c \u2192 NOT (has C)\n- aBc: a and c \u2192 offspring is a/a; B/b; c/c \u2192 YES, a/a;c/c\n\nSo the gamete classes that produce a/a;c/c offspring are: **abc, Abc, aBc**\n\nWait, let me recheck. The condition is a/a AND c/c.\n\n- abc: a from heterozygous parent, c from heterozygous parent \u2192 a/a and c/c \u2192 YES\n- Abc: a from heterozygous parent, c from heterozygous parent \u2192 a/a and c/c \u2192 YES\n- aBc: a from heterozygous parent, c from heterozygous parent \u2192 a/a and c/c \u2192 YES\n\nSo the affected classes are: abc, Abc, aBc\n\nTheir observed counts: 170, 80, 4\n\nSince they survive with probability 1/2, the original zygote numbers would be:\n- abc: 170 \u00d7 2 = 340\n- Abc: 80 \u00d7 2 = 160\n- aBc: 4 \u00d7 2 = 8\n\nThe unaffected classes keep their counts:\n- ABC: 344\n- aBC: 80\n- ABc: 70\n- abC: 70\n- AbC: 8\n\nCorrected total: 344 + 340 + 160 + 80 + 70 + 70 + 8 + 8 = 1080\n\nLet me verify: 344 + 340 = 684; 684 + 160 = 844; 844 + 80 = 924; 924 + 70 = 994; 994 + 70 = 1064; 1064 + 8 = 1072; 1072 + 8 = 1080. \u2713\n\n**Step 2: Identify parental and double-crossover classes, determine gene order**\n\nParental haplotypes are ABC and abc.\n\nParental (non-recombinant) classes: ABC and abc\n- ABC: 344\n- abc: 340\n\nThese are the two largest classes, which confirms they are the parental classes.\n\nNow I need to determine gene order. The three possible orders are:\n- A-B-C\n- A-C-B\n- B-A-C\n\nThe double-crossover classes are the rarest classes. Let me look at the corrected counts:\n- ABC: 344\n- abc: 340\n- Abc: 160\n- aBC: 80\n- ABc: 70\n- abC: 70\n- AbC: 8\n- aBc: 8\n\nThe rarest classes are AbC (8) and aBc (8). These should be the double-crossover classes.\n\nFor a double crossover, the middle gene gets \"switched\" relative to the parental configuration.\n\nIf the order is A-B-C:\n- Parental: ABC and abc\n- A single crossover between A and B: aBC and Abc (wait, let me be more careful)\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. The parental chromosomes are ABC and abc.\n\nIf the order is A-B-C:\n- No crossover: ABC, abc\n- Single crossover between A and B: aBC, Abc (the left part comes from one chromosome, the right part from the other)\n- Single crossover between B and C: ABc, abC\n- Double crossover (both intervals): AbC, aBc\n\nIf the order is A-C-B:\n- No crossover: ACB... wait, I need to be careful about the order of genes.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The gametes are listed as (A locus, B locus, C locus). The gene order on the chromosome determines which genes are adjacent.\n\nIf the gene order is A-B-C (A and B are adjacent, B and C are adjacent):\n- Parental: ABC, abc\n- SCO in interval A-B: aBC, Abc\n- SCO in interval B-C: ABc, abC\n- DCO: AbC, aBc\n\nIf the gene order is B-A-C (B and A are adjacent, A and C are adjacent):\n- The chromosomes in order B-A-C are: BAC and bac\n- Parental: BAC, bac \u2192 in our notation: ABC, abc\n- SCO in interval B-A: bAC, Bac \u2192 in our notation: aBC, Abc... \n\nWait, I need to be more careful. Let me write the chromosomes in the order they appear on the chromosome.\n\nIf gene order is A-B-C:\nChromosome 1: A - B - C\nChromosome 2: a - b - c\n\nSCO between A and B: a - B - C and A - b - c \u2192 gametes: aBC, Abc\nSCO between B and C: A - B - c and a - b - C \u2192 gametes: ABc, abC\nDCO: A - b - C and a - B - c \u2192 gametes: AbC, aBc\n\nIf gene order is B-A-C:\nChromosome 1: B - A - C\nChromosome 2: b - a - c\n\nSCO between B and A: b - A - C and B - a - c \u2192 gametes: aBC, Abc (in ABC notation)\nWait: bAC in ABC notation is aBC (A locus = A, B locus = b, C locus = C)... \n\nHmm, I'm getting confused. Let me be very explicit.\n\nThe gamete is described as (allele at A, allele at B, allele at C).\n\nIf the physical order on the chromosome is B - A - C:\nChromosome 1: B - A - C (meaning the B locus has allele B, A locus has allele A, C locus has allele C)\nChromosome 2: b - a - c\n\nSCO between B and A: \n- Left of crossover from chr 1, right from chr 2: b - a - c \u2192 wait no.\n- A crossover between B and A means: the part to the left of the crossover (including B) comes from one chromosome, and the part to the right (including A and C) comes from the other.\n\nLet me use a different approach. A crossover between two adjacent genes swaps the segments. \n\nIf order is B - A - C:\n- Parental: (B,A,C) and (b,a,c)\n- Crossover between B and A: (b,A,C) and (B,a,c) \u2192 in (A,B,C) notation: (A,b,C) and (a,B,c) \u2192 AbC and aBc\n- Crossover between A and C: (B,a,c) and (b,A,C) \u2192 in (A,B,C) notation: (a,B,c) and (A,b,C) \u2192 aBc and AbC\n\nWait, that gives the same result? That can't be right. Let me redo this.\n\nIf order is B - A - C:\nChromosome 1: B --- A --- C\nChromosome 2: b --- a --- c\n\nCrossover between B and A (i.e., in the interval between B and A):\n- Recombinant 1: b (from chr 2, left of crossover) + A, C (from chr 1, right of crossover) = b, A, C\n- Recombinant 2: B (from chr 1, left of crossover) + a, c (from chr 2, right of crossover) = B, a, c\n\nIn (A, B, C) notation:\n- b, A, C \u2192 A = A, B = b, C = C \u2192 AbC\n- B, a, c \u2192 A = a, B = B, C = c \u2192 aBc\n\nCrossover between A and C (i.e., in the interval between A and C):\n- Recombinant 1: B, A (from chr 1, left of crossover) + c (from chr 2, right of crossover) = B, A, c\n- Recombinant 2: b, a (from chr 2, left of crossover) + C (from chr 1, right of crossover) = b, a, C\n\nIn (A, B, C) notation:\n- B, A, c \u2192 A = A, B = B, C = c \u2192 ABc\n- b, a, C \u2192 A = a, B = b, C = C \u2192 abC\n\nDCO (crossovers in both intervals):\n- B (chr 1) + a (chr 2) + C (chr 1) = B, a, C \u2192 in (A,B,C): aBc\n- b (chr 2) + A (chr 1) + c (chr 2) = b, A, c \u2192 in (A,B,C): AbC\n\nWait, that gives the same DCO classes. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually wait. For DCO, we need crossovers in both intervals. Let me be more careful.\n\nIf order is B - A - C:\nPositions: B --- A --- C\nChr 1: B --- A --- C\nChr 2: b --- a --- c\n\nDCO means crossover between B and A, AND crossover between A and C.\n\nStarting from the left:\n- Before first crossover (left of B-A interval): take from chr 1 \u2192 B\n- Between first and second crossover (the A gene): take from chr 2 \u2192 a\n- After second crossover (right of A-C interval, the C gene): take from chr 1 \u2192 C\n\nSo one DCO product: B, a, C \u2192 in (A,B,C) notation: A=a, B=B, C=C \u2192 aBC\n\nWait, that's not right either. Let me be even more careful.\n\nThe chromosome has three genes in order B, A, C. A crossover occurs between two adjacent genes.\n\nChr 1: [B] --- [A] --- [C]\nChr 2: [b] --- [a] --- [c]\n\nFor a DCO (crossover in both intervals):\n- Segment left of first crossover (which is between B and A): comes from one chromosome\n- Middle segment (which is just gene A): comes from the other chromosome\n- Segment right of second crossover (which is between A and C): comes from the first chromosome again (alternating)\n\nSo:\nDCO product 1: B (from chr 1) + a (from chr 2) + C (from chr 1)\nIn (A,B,C) notation: A = a, B = B, C = C \u2192 aBC\n\nDCO product 2: b (from chr 2) + A (from chr 1) + c (from chr 2)\nIn (A,B,C) notation: A = A, B = b, C = c \u2192 Abc\n\nHmm, so if the order is B-A-C, the DCO classes would be aBC and Abc.\n\nLet me check: in our data, the rarest classes are AbC (8) and aBc (8).\n\nIf order is A-B-C: DCO classes are AbC and aBc \u2713 (matches the rarest)\nIf order is B-A-C: DCO classes are aBC and Abc (these have counts 80 and 160, not the rarest)\nIf order is A-C-B: Let me check this.\n\nIf order is A - C - B:\nChr 1: [A] --- [C] --- [B]\nChr 2: [a] --- [c] --- [b]\n\nSCO between A and C:\n- a (chr 2) + C, B (chr 1) \u2192 a, C, B \u2192 in (A,B,C): A=a, B=B, C=C \u2192 aBC\n- A (chr 1) + c, b (chr 2) \u2192 A, c, b \u2192 in (A,B,C): A=A, B=b, C=c \u2192 Abc\n\nSCO between C and B:\n- A, C (chr 1) + b (chr 2) \u2192 A, C, b \u2192 in (A,B,C): A=A, B=b, C=C \u2192 AbC\n- a, c (chr 2) + B (chr 1) \u2192 a, c, B \u2192 in (A,B,C): A=a, B=B, C=c \u2192 aBc\n\nDCO:\n- A (chr 1) + c (chr 2) + B (chr 1) \u2192 A, c, B \u2192 in (A,B,C): A=A, B=B, C=c \u2192 ABc\n- a (chr 2) + C (chr 1) + b (chr 2) \u2192 a, C, b \u2192 in (A,B,C): A=a, B=b, C=C \u2192 abC\n\nSo if order is A-C-B: DCO classes are ABc and abC (counts 70 and 70, not the rarest)\n\nTherefore, the gene order must be **A - B - C**, since the DCO classes (AbC and aBc) are the rarest.\n\nLet me verify the full classification for order A-B-C:\n\nParental: ABC (344), abc (340)\nSCO in interval 1 (A-B): Abc (160), aBC (80)\nSCO in interval 2 (B-C): ABc (70), abC (70)\nDCO: AbC (8), aBc (8)\n\n**Step 3: Calculate recombination fractions and map distances**\n\nTotal corrected offspring: 1080\n\nInterval 1 (A-B): Recombinants are Abc, aBC, AbC, aBc\nWait, I need to think about what counts as a recombinant in each interval.\n\nFor interval A-B (the interval between genes A and B):\n- Parental configuration: A-B and a-b (i.e., ABC and abc have A linked with B, a linked with b)\n- Recombinant for A-B: Ab and aB (i.e., A linked with b, or a linked with B)\n\nSo recombinant gametes for A-B interval:\n- Abc: A and b \u2192 recombinant for A-B \u2713\n- aBC: a and B \u2192 recombinant for A-B \u2713\n- AbC: A and b \u2192 recombinant for A-B \u2713\n- aBc: a and B \u2192 recombinant for A-B \u2713\n\nNumber of A-B recombinants: 160 + 80 + 8 + 8 = 256\n\nRecombination fraction for A-B: 256/1080 = 0.23704...\n\nHmm, let me double-check. The recombination fraction between A and B is the proportion of gametes that show recombination between A and B.\n\nGametes where A and B are in coupling (parental arrangement): ABC, Abc, abC, abc\nWait no. Let me think again.\n\nParental chromosomes: A-B-C and a-b-c\nSo in the parental configuration, A is always with B (both uppercase or both lowercase at A and B positions).\n\nGametes with parental A-B arrangement: ABC (A with B), Abc (a with b), abC (a with b), abc (a with b)\nWait: ABC has A and B (both capital) \u2192 parental\nAbc has A and b \u2192 recombinant for A-B\nabC has a and b \u2192 parental\nabc has a and b \u2192 parental\n\nHmm wait, I think I'm confusing myself. Let me restart.\n\nThe two parental chromosomes are:\n- Chromosome 1: A - B - C\n- Chromosome 2: a - b - c\n\nA gamete is \"recombinant in the A-B interval\" if the alleles at A and B come from different parental chromosomes.\n\nFor each gamete, I check whether the A allele and B allele are from the same parent:\n-