8 = 1600/3698 = 800/1849 \u2248 0.4327\n\nInterference = 1 - 0.4327 = 0.5673\n\n**Step 6: Uncorrected calculations**\n\nUsing observed counts directly:\n- ABC: 344\n- abc: 170\n- Abc: 80\n- aBC: 80\n- ABc: 70\n- abC: 70\n- AbC: 8\n- aBc: 4\n\nTotal observed = 344 + 170 + 80 + 80 + 70 + 70 + 8 + 4 = 826\n\nGene order is still A-B-C (the rarest classes are still AbC and aBc).\n\nA-B recombinants: Abc (80) + aBC (80) + AbC (8) + aBc (4) = 172\nRF(A-B) = 172/826 = 0.20823... \u2248 0.2082\n\nB-C recombinants: ABc (70) + abC (70) + AbC (8) + aBc (4) = 152\nRF(B-C) = 152/826 = 0.18402... \u2248 0.1840\n\nA-C recombinants: Abc (80) + aBC (80) + ABc (70) + abC (70) = 300\nRF(A-C) = 300/826 = 0.36320... \u2248 0.3632\n\nExpected DCO (uncorrected) = (172/826) \u00d7 (152/826) \u00d7 826 = 172 \u00d7 152 / 826 = 26144/826 = 31.65\n\nObserved DCO (uncorrected) = 8 + 4 = 12\n\nc.o.c. = 12/31.65 = 0.3792\n\nInterference = 1 - 0.3792 = 0.6208\n\nLet me double-check: 172 \u00d7 152 = 172 \u00d7 150 + 172 \u00d7 2 = 25800 + 344 = 26144\n26144/826 = 31.65...\n\nc.o.c. = 12/31.65 = 0.3792\nInterference = 0.6208\n\n**Step 7: Comparison and explanation**\n\nCorrected:\n- RF(A-B) = 256/1080 = 0.2370 (23.70 cM)\n- RF(B-C) = 156/1080 = 0.1444 (14.44 cM)\n- RF(A-C) = 380/1080 = 0.3519 (35.19 cM)\n- c.o.c. = 0.4327\n- Interference = 0.5673\n\nUncorrected:\n- RF(A-B) = 172/826 = 0.2082 (20.82 cM)\n- RF(B-C) = 152/826 = 0.1840 (18.40 cM)\n- RF(A-C) = 300/826 = 0.3632 (36.32 cM)\n- c.o.c. = 0.3792\n- Interference = 0.6208\n\nThe viability selection affects the abc, Abc, and aBc classes (reducing them by half). \n\nLet me see how this distorts the estimates:\n\nThe affected classes are:\n- abc (parental): 340 \u2192 170 (reduced by 170)\n- Abc (SCO in interval 1, A-B): 160 \u2192 80 (reduced by 80)\n- aBc (DCO): 8 \u2192 4 (reduced by 4)\n\nThe unaffected classes are:\n- ABC (parental): 344\n- aBC (SCO in interval 1, A-B): 80\n- ABc (SCO in interval 2, B-C): 70\n- abC (SCO in interval 2, B-C): 70\n- AbC (DCO): 8\n\nSo the selection:\n1. Reduces the parental class abc by half \u2192 this makes the other parental class ABC appear relatively more abundant, but since both are parental, the total parental count is reduced.\n2. Reduces Abc (SCO in A-B interval) by half \u2192 this makes the A-B interval appear to have fewer recombinants, reducing the apparent RF(A-B).\n3. Reduces aBc (DCO) by half \u2192 this reduces the apparent DCO count.\n\nThe key distortion:\n- The Abc class (SCO in interval 1) is hit by selection, but its reciprocal aBC is not. This makes the A-B interval appear to have asymmetric recombination, and reduces the apparent RF(A-B) because one of the two SCO classes in that interval is reduced.\n- The abc class (parental) is hit, but ABC is not. This doesn't directly affect recombination fractions (since both are parental), but it affects the total and the relative proportions.\n- The aBc class (DCO) is hit, but AbC is not. This makes the DCO classes appear asymmetric and reduces the apparent DCO count.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully in terms of how it affects the recombination fractions.\n\nFor RF(A-B):\nCorrected: (160 + 80 + 8 + 8)/1080 = 256/1080\nUncorrected: (80 + 80 + 8 + 4)/826 = 172/826\n\nThe numerator decreased by 84 (160-80 + 8-4 = 80+4 = 84)\nThe denominator decreased by 254 (1080-826)\n\nThe effect on the ratio depends on the relative changes. Since 84/256 = 0.328 and 254/1080 = 0.235, the numerator decreased proportionally more than the denominator, so the ratio decreased.\n\nFor RF(B-C):\nCorrected: (70 + 70 + 8 + 8)/1080 = 156/1080\nUncorrected: (70 + 70 + 8 + 4)/826 = 152/826\n\nThe numerator decreased by 4 (only aBc went from 8 to 4)\nThe denominator decreased by 254\n\nSince 4/156 = 0.0256 and 254/1080 = 0.235, the denominator decreased proportionally much more than the numerator, so the ratio increased.\n\nThis is the key insight: the viability selection differentially affects the reciprocal classes within each interval. For the A-B interval, one SCO class (Abc) is reduced but the other (aBC) is not. For the B-C interval, one DCO class (aBc) is reduced but the other (AbC) is not.\n\nThis creates an apparent distortion in the recombination pattern that mimics what you might see if recombination were not symmetric (e.g., if there were chromosome interference or sex-limited recombination differences), but is actually an artifact of post-fertilization selection.\n\nLet me recalculate more carefully to make sure my numbers are right.\n\nCorrected data:\n| Gamete | Corrected |\n|--------|-----------|\n| ABC    | 344       |\n| abc    | 340       |\n| Abc    | 160       |\n| aBC    | 80        |\n| ABc    | 70        |\n| abC    | 70        |\n| AbC    | 8         |\n| aBc    | 8         |\nTotal    | 1080      |\n\nUncorrected (observed) data:\n| Gamete | Observed |\n|--------|----------|\n| ABC    | 344      |\n| abc    | 170      |\n| Abc    | 80       |\n| aBC    | 80       |\n| ABc    | 70       |\n| abC    | 70       |\n| AbC    | 8        |\n| aBc    | 4        |\nTotal    | 826      |\n\nNow let me present this in a clear, organized way.\n\nActually, I want to double-check my identification of which gametes produce a/a; c/c offspring.\n\nThe testcross is: (heterozygous female) \u00d7 (abc/abc male)\n\nThe female produces gametes. The male produces only abc gametes.\n\nOffspring genotype = (female gamete) / (abc)\n\nSo:\n- If female gamete is ABC: offspring is ABC/abc \u2192 A/a, B/b, C/c \u2192 not a/a;c/c\n- If female gamete is abc: offspring is abc/abc \u2192 a/a, b/b, c/c \u2192 IS a/a;c/c \u2713\n- If female gamete is Abc: offspring is Abc/abc \u2192 a/a, B/b, c/c \u2192 IS a/a;c/c \u2713\n- If female gamete is aBC: offspring is aBC/abc \u2192 a/a, B/b, C/c \u2192 NOT a/a;c/c (has C/c)\n- If female gamete is ABc: offspring is ABc/abc \u2192 A/a, B/b, c/c \u2192 NOT a/a;c/c (has A/a)\n- If female gamete is abC: offspring is abC/abc \u2192 a/a, b/b, C/c \u2192